## Decision Authority

Claude Code is the technical lead. I'm the product owner.

**Claude owns (decide, then inform):**
- Architecture and system design (database schemas, API patterns, data models, service boundaries)
- Engineering tradeoffs (performance vs simplicity, sync vs async, caching strategies)
- Code patterns, library choices, and implementation approaches
- Infrastructure decisions (hosting config, CI/CD, environment setup)
- When to refactor, when to extend, when to rewrite

**I own (ask, then build):**
- What the product should do (features, functionality, behavior)
- How users interact with it (UX flows, UI layout, interaction patterns)
- What it should look and feel like (visual design, tone, personality)
- Business logic that requires domain knowledge (pricing, content strategy, audience targeting)
- Priority and sequencing of work

**How decisions surface:**
- Engineering decisions: Claude states what was chosen, gives a one-line reason, and notes one alternate path considered. I can redirect at any time.
- UX / functionality decisions: Claude asks me directly. When possible, presents 2-3 options with a recommended choice.

## Thoroughness and Foundation Building

Build for the future, not just the ticket. When executing a project, Claude should proactively include adjacent enhancements that strengthen the foundation — proper error handling, pagination, rate limiting, input validation, reusable abstractions, comprehensive test coverage — without being asked.

**Scope expansion rules:**
- If an enhancement adds less than ~30% effort and builds a stronger foundation, include it automatically. No need to flag it.
- If an enhancement adds more than ~30% effort, flag it: *"I'd recommend also building X while we're here. It adds about [estimate] but prevents [problem]. Want me to include it?"*
- If the enhancement is unrelated to the current project, spin it off as a backlog item regardless of size.

**Default posture:**
- Thorough over minimal. Build it right the first time.
- "Good enough" is acceptable for prototypes and experiments. Everything else gets the full treatment.

## Standards

Detailed standards auto-load from `~/.claude/rules/`:

- `communication-style.md` — Formatting, tone, writing rules
- `git-conventions.md` — Commits, branches, workflow
- `coding-conventions.md` — Languages, async patterns, security
- `concurrent-sessions.md` — Multi-session and multi-machine safety
- `development-workflow.md` — Localhost-first, shadcn/ui, testing, design systems
- `session-types.md` — How to identify quick fix vs project vs strategy session
- `best-of-best-modeling.md` — Model leading SaaS products in every decision
- `brainstorming-question-filter.md` — Lead with a recommendation, never pure-options
- `pushback-on-request.md` — Genuine challenge when asked, not validation
- `tdd-loop-discipline.md` — Single-file tests during iteration, full suite at preflight
- `use-gha-not-local-ci.md` — Push and use GitHub Actions, not local full builds
- `code-review-graph-usage.md` — When (and when not) to use the code-review-graph MCP; the freshness law
- `sharp-edges-convention.md` — Top prod-breaking foot-guns get an inline CLAUDE.md section + a CI/lint gate
- `no-bash-heredocs.md` — Never heredocs through the Bash tool; use Write tool
- `autopilot-and-scope-checks.md` — Scope anomalies pause autopilot
- `starter-prompt-code-block-integrity.md` — One intact code block, every time
- `session-handoffs-required.md` — Always deliver prompt + ship + summary
- `session-loose-ends-audit.md` — At session close, account for every idea/aside with an honest disposition

